 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Drive current and grey scale",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "This is the 12 mA version, 5 V at 12 mA per channel. With one colour on, all 256 grey levels may be used; with red, green and blue on together, use up to 70 % of the grey scale."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "An internal reset circuit holds all three LEDs off when power is applied."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data coding",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The line uses unipolar return-to-zero coding. Each bit begins high and must end low; a short high (T0H) is a 0 and a long high (T1H) a 1. The bit period is at least 1.2 µs. A low level held longer than 200 µs is the reset code that separates one refresh cycle from the next."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Chaining",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The controller sends one 24-bit word per LED back to back. The first LED keeps the first word and regenerates the remainder on DOUT for the second, which keeps the next word, and so on down the chain. After a reset the first LED again takes the first word."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Colour order",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Each 24-bit word is green, red, blue, eight bits each, most significant bit first."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data rate and chain length",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The line runs at up to 800 Kbps. At a 30 frame per second refresh, fewer than 1024 LEDs can be cascaded."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Optics",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Translucent lens, 120° typical viewing angle at half intensity."
  },
  {
